'''Companyia Grand Balboa, S.A.''' ({{lang|ca|Companyia Grand Balboa}}), commonly known as '''CGB''', is a Balboan diversified industrial holding company and the largest industrial conglomerate in the country. Headquartered in the [[Districte Financer (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Financer]] of [[Sant Cristòfor]], the company operates across three divisions — construction materials, industrial equipment, and consumer products — and is the largest domestically owned manufacturer in Balboa. It is listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] under the ticker CGB.

CGB was founded in 1963 as a cement manufacturer by [[Francesc Prat i Soler]], a civil engineer who had worked in the mandate-era public works department. The company diversified through a series of acquisitions in the 1970s and 1980s, expanding from cement into steel, glass, industrial machinery, and consumer appliances, and establishing itself as the dominant industrial conglomerate in the post-reunification Balboan economy.

The company's name — "Grand Balboa" — was chosen by Francesc Prat to reflect his ambition to build a company of national scale, and the slightly unconventional use of "Grand" (rather than the Catalan "Gran") was a deliberate branding choice that the founder felt gave the company a more distinctive, memorable identity.

== History ==

=== Founding (1963) ===

Companyia Grand Balboa was founded in 1963 by [[Francesc Prat i Soler]], a civil engineer who had worked in the public works department of the [[International Mandate of Sant Cristòfor]]. Prat had been responsible for sourcing construction materials for the mandate-era infrastructure programme, and he had observed that the Balboan isthmus imported most of its cement from Volisania at significant cost. Recognising that the post-reunification construction boom would generate strong demand for domestically produced cement, Prat left the public works department and established the '''Ciment de Balboa''' cement plant in Sant Bart, the first large-scale cement manufacturing facility in the interior.

The plant was financed with Prat's savings, a bank loan, and an investment from his wife's family, the Soler family, who were interior landowners with interests in the agricultural trade. The cement plant began production in 1964 and was profitable within its first year, as the post-reunification construction boom generated strong demand.

=== Expansion into construction materials (1963–1980) ===

The company's early growth was driven by the post-reunification construction boom. The reunification of Balboa in 1960 and the subsequent economic expansion produced a surge in demand for construction materials — cement, steel, glass, and bricks — as the interior cities modernised and the Sant Cristòfor metropolitan area expanded.

Prat expanded the cement business in the late 1960s, building a second plant in the Estret corridor and acquiring a small steel fabrication shop in Sant Cristòfor in 1968. The steel acquisition — originally intended to produce reinforcing bars for the construction business — marked the company's first step beyond cement and into the broader construction materials sector.

In 1972, the company acquired '''Vidres de l'Istme''', a glass manufacturer in Portnou that produced window glass and glass containers. The acquisition gave CGB a presence in the glass market and expanded its geographic reach beyond the interior. By 1975, the company operated three cement plants, two steel fabrication facilities, and the Vidres de l'Istme glass works, and it had become the largest construction materials company in Balboa.

The company was incorporated as Companyia Grand Balboa, S.A. in 1974, adopting the name that Francesc Prat had envisioned for the diversified industrial company he intended to build. The cement business was retained as the Ciment de Balboa division, and the company began planning its diversification beyond construction materials.

=== Diversification into industrial equipment (1980–1995) ===

The 1980s saw CGB diversify into industrial equipment, driven by Francesc Prat's recognition that the Balboan economy was importing most of its industrial machinery from Volisania and that there was a market for a domestic manufacturer. The company's first industrial equipment acquisition was '''Maquinària de l'Estret''' (1981), a small manufacturer of agricultural processing equipment in the Estret corridor that produced mills, conveyors, and packaging machines for the interior cooperatives and the food processing companies.

The Maquinària de l'Estret acquisition was followed by the establishment of '''CGB Industrial''' (1985), a division that manufactured industrial pumps, valves, and piping for the canal corridor's oil and chemical industries. The division was established in response to the growth of the Balboan oil and chemical sector, which had previously relied on imported equipment.

In 1989, CGB acquired '''Astilleros de Portnou''', a small shipyard in Portnou that specialised in repair and maintenance of coastal vessels and canal barges. The acquisition gave CGB a presence in the maritime engineering sector and provided a service that was in demand from the canal's shipping traffic.

The [[Balboa real-estate asset crisis]] and the [[1994 Balboa spot recession]] tested the company's resilience. The construction materials division was hit hard by the collapse of the real-estate market, but the industrial equipment division — which served the agricultural and oil sectors — was less affected. The company's diversification, which had been driven by ambition, proved to be its principal protection against the downturn. CGB acquired several distressed manufacturing operations from failed companies at favourable prices during the recession, strengthening its position for the recovery.

The company was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 1995, with the Prat family retaining a 35 per cent stake. The listing raised capital that was used to modernise the company's manufacturing facilities and to fund the expansion into consumer products.

=== Diversification into consumer products (1995–2010) ===

The late 1990s and 2000s saw CGB diversify into consumer products, driven by the growth of the Balboan middle class and the increasing demand for domestically produced consumer goods. The company's first consumer products acquisition was '''Electrodomèstics de Balboa''' (1997), a manufacturer of household appliances — refrigerators, washing machines, and cookers — that was based in Sant Cristòfor and had been struggling to compete with imported Volisanian brands.

CGB invested in modernising Electrodomèstics de Balboa's production facilities and developing a brand strategy that emphasised Balboan origin and quality. The rebranded '''CGB Electrodomèstics''' division became the largest domestic appliance brand in Balboa, capturing approximately 30 per cent of the domestic market by 2010.

In 2003, CGB acquired '''Envasos de l'Istme''', a packaging manufacturer that produced plastic containers, glass bottles, and metal cans for the food and beverage industry. The acquisition was a natural extension of the company's construction materials and glass operations, and it gave CGB a presence in the packaging supply chain for the food processing companies, including [[Hirocorp, S.A.|Hirocorp]].

=== Modern era (2010–present) ===

The 2010s saw CGB continue to expand its operations, with a focus on modernising its manufacturing facilities and developing export markets for its industrial equipment and consumer products. The company established export relationships for its industrial equipment division in Tapuya and Sinchew, where the growing industrial sectors created demand for affordable machinery.

The [[Adonian pandemic]] of 2020 had a mixed impact on CGB. The construction materials division was affected by the temporary halt in construction activity, but the consumer products division benefited from the surge in demand for household appliances as consumers spent more time at home. The industrial equipment division was stable, as the agricultural and oil sectors remained operational.

By 2026, CGB employed approximately 28,000 people across its three divisions and operated manufacturing facilities in Sant Cristòfor, Portnou, Sant Bart, and the Estret corridor. The Prat family retained a 24 per cent stake, and the company remained the largest domestically owned manufacturer in Balboa.

== Operations ==

CGB operates across three principal divisions:

* '''CGB Construction Materials''': The company's original and largest division, producing cement, steel, glass, and bricks for the Balboan construction industry. The division operates three cement plants (Sant Bart, Estret, and Portnou), two steel fabrication facilities (Sant Cristòfor and Estret), and the Vidres de l'Istme glass works (Portnou). The division is the largest supplier of construction materials in Balboa.
* '''CGB Industrial Equipment''': The division manufactures agricultural processing equipment, industrial pumps and valves, and packaging machinery, and operates the Astilleros de Portnou shipyard. The division serves the agricultural, oil and chemical, and maritime sectors, and has growing export sales to Tapuya and Sinchew.
* '''CGB Consumer Products''': The division manufactures household appliances under the CGB Electrodomèstics brand and packaging products through Envasos de l'Istme. The appliance brand is the largest domestic brand in Balboa, with approximately 30 per cent of the domestic market, and the packaging division supplies the food and beverage industry.

The company's headquarters are at 300 Carrer Frederic, in the Districte Financer of Sant Cristòfor, and it maintains divisional headquarters in Sant Bart (construction materials), Estret (industrial equipment), and Portnou (consumer products).

